/**
@page board_lm3s6965evb LM3S6965EVB (QEMU)
@tableofcontents

<div class="hc-board-header">
<div>
The `lm3s6965evb` target is the development and regression platform. It runs
in QEMU's `lm3s6965evb` machine, which emulates TI's Stellaris LM3S6965
evaluation board. Every CI run builds it and runs the command regression
against it.
<div class="hc-badges"><span class="hc-badge hc-ok">QEMU tested in CI</span><span class="hc-badge hc-info">Emulator only</span></div>
The port relies on QEMU's emulated reset state. It does not configure real
GPIO, UART baud rates, or a physical oscillator, so physical Stellaris
bring-up is not covered.
</div>
<div class="hc-spec-panel">
<span class="hc-card-title">At a glance</span>
<table class="hc-specs">
<tr><th>SoC</th><td>TI LM3S6965 (`ti/lm3s6965`)</td></tr>
<tr><th>Core</th><td>Cortex-M3</td></tr>
<tr><th>Flash</th><td>256 KB at `0x00000000`</td></tr>
<tr><th>RAM</th><td>64 KB at `0x20000000`</td></tr>
<tr><th>Clock</th><td>12.5 MHz reported, no clock setup</td></tr>
<tr><th>Console</th><td>UART0 through QEMU serial</td></tr>
<tr><th>Presets</th><td>`lm3s6965evb`, `lm3s6965evb-release`</td></tr>
</table>
</div>
</div>

@section board_lm3s6965evb_run Build and run

```bash
cmake --preset lm3s6965evb -DPython3_EXECUTABLE="$PWD/.venv/bin/python"
cmake --build --preset lm3s6965evb
bash scripts/run-qemu-lm3s6965evb.sh
```

The script boots `build/lm3s6965evb/homecore` with `-nographic`. Exit with
Ctrl-A, then X. Try `help`, `uptime`, `cat /dev/uptime`, `mem`, the file
commands (also under `/ram`, a littlefs volume on a 32 KB ramdisk), and
`reboot`. Reboot discards RAM files and the `/ram` contents and resets the
session. See @ref storage.

For the non-interactive regression, run:

```bash
python3 tests/qemu_files_test.py
```

@section board_lm3s6965evb_console Console and devices

@htmlinclude[block] lm3s6965evb-qemu.svg

The SoC registers `/dev/uart0`, `/dev/uart1`, and `/dev/uart2`. Standard input,
output, and error use UART0. The other nodes are not additional shell sessions.

QEMU's machine has no LED, so `/dev/led0` is a console LED
(`homecore,console-led`): each change prints `[led0] on` or `[led0] off`, and
reads return the last state set. The physical board's status LED on PF0 is not
supported. See @ref led.

@section board_lm3s6965evb_clock Clock and uptime

`board_cpu_clock_hz()` returns 12.5 MHz, which matches QEMU's Stellaris reset
divider configuration; see the
[QEMU model](https://github.com/qemu/qemu/blob/master/hw/arm/stellaris.c).
Uptime follows guest virtual time and stops while emulation is paused. Update
the clock reporting if clock setup or physical hardware support is added.

@section board_lm3s6965evb_memory Memory

@htmlinclude[block] lm3s6965evb-memory.svg

The main stack uses the default `CONFIG_HOMECORE_KERNEL_MAIN_STACK_SIZE` of
3 KB. `mem` reports heap use and the stack high-water mark.

@section board_lm3s6965evb_limits Limitations

- QEMU success verifies this emulated target only. It does not verify STM32
  register programming or UART wiring.
- The port is not suitable for a physical LM3S6965EVB without clock, GPIO, and
  UART initialization.

The full board guide is
[docs/boards/lm3s6965evb.md](https://github.com/grzegorz-grzeda/home-core/blob/main/docs/boards/lm3s6965evb.md).
*/
